window.docContents[146].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity":{"contents":"The ball and half-space constructions provide the weak maximum principle and\nPoisson uniqueness used by the checked mean-value argument. Mean values then\nlead to derivative estimates, stronger maximum principles, Harnack\ncomparison, and Liouville rigidity.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations","header":"5.3. Model Dirichlet Problems and Harmonic Rigidity","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Time-Traces-and-Integrated-Solutions":{"contents":"An element of  is an almost-everywhere equivalence class, so its\nvalue at a particular time has no meaning, and an equation in  almost\neverywhere does not by itself license writing u 0. Parabolic initial\nconditions and energy identities nevertheless require endpoint values.\nLions–Magenes regularity resolves this for a trajectory square integrable in\n whose weak time derivative is square integrable in . Time\nmollification proves the energy identity for smooth approximants, and the\nlimit selects a unique continuous -valued representative satisfying the\nenergy identity. This representative carries the initial trace and the\npointwise-in-time energy estimates.\n\nThe integrated formulation moves the time derivative from the trajectory onto\na scalar time test. It makes sense directly for a Bochner  class and\nrecords the initial datum through the boundary term created by integration by\nparts. It is well suited to weak convergence because it avoids selecting a\npointwise derivative for each approximation, and the Galerkin limit is taken\nin this formulation. When the form is strongly measurable and essentially\nbounded, a residual theorem recovers  as the weak \ntime derivative, Lions–Magenes supplies the continuous representative with\nits initial trace, and the energy identity gives uniqueness and continuous\ndependence under coercivity or a Gårding bound.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Weak Evolution\u0009Weak Evolution and Galerkin Approximation","header":"6.3.1.3. Time Traces and Integrated Solutions","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Time-Traces-and-Integrated-Solutions"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Weak-Derivatives-and-Approximation":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives","header":"4.2. Weak Derivatives and Approximation","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Weak-Derivatives-and-Approximation"}});